From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:55:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/h264: skip a dependent view while the context is not
 set up

A slice of a dependent view (a coded slice extension) does not set the
decoding context up: h264_init_ps() checks that its subset SPS agrees
with what the base view set up, and returns. After a flush the context
is not set up (h264_decode_flush() frees its tables); the next slice of
the base view does that again.

The series takes that slice to come first. In a file that keeps the
dependent view of an access unit in the packet of the base view of the
next one (the layout that commit 0014 of the series describes for some
Matroska files), the first slice after a flush is one of a dependent
view. Its picture was started on a context without tables, and the
decoder wrote through a null pointer. A player flushes at every seek,
and when it has drained the decoder because its input ran dry.

Such a slice is now skipped, as the slice of a view that is not decoded
is, and counted as such (skipped_view_slices), so that a packet that
holds nothing else is not an error. Nothing is lost by it: the base
view that it is predicted from was in the packet before the flush.

Measured with the series as of 2026-10-14, carried to FFmpeg 9.0.2, by
driving the decoder as a player does: the packets of the 20 streams of
two views at hand (19 of the ITU-T H.264.1 MVC conformance set and one
made with the reference encoder), one access unit a packet and in that
layout, with a flush before the second packet, at a third or at two
thirds of the stream, with 1, 8 and 16 frame threads and 8 slice
threads. Without this change 240 of the 480 runs end in a segmentation
fault: all of them in that layout. With it none does, and every picture
that comes out is, byte for byte, the one that the decode of the same
packets without a flush gives for that packet and view. The exception
is one stream in that layout, with or without a flush: MVCICT-1, which
is field coded, gives more pictures of its second view there than with
one access unit a packet (the two fields of a picture are then in
different packets). The series of August does the same, so neither
this change nor the new series brings it.
---
diff --git a/libavcodec/h264_slice.c b/libavcodec/h264_slice.c
--- a/libavcodec/h264_slice.c
+++ b/libavcodec/h264_slice.c
@@ -2549,6 +2549,18 @@ int ff_h264_queue_decode_slice(H264Context *h, const H2645NAL *nal)
             h->skipped_view_slices = 1;
             return 0;
         }
+        if (!h->context_initialized) {
+            /* No slice of the base view has set the context up, at the start
+             * or since a flush: h264_init_ps() does not do that for a
+             * dependent view, and its picture would be started on a context
+             * without tables. The base view to predict this slice from is not
+             * there either: where a packet holds the dependent view of one
+             * access unit and the base view of the next, it was in the packet
+             * before. */
+            sl->ref_count[0] = sl->ref_count[1] = 0;
+            h->skipped_view_slices = 1;
+            return 0;
+        }
         view = voidx;
     } else
         view = 0;
